{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And whoever touches upon the face of the field.</b> Since there is no tent there, it defiles only by contact." "<b>In the slain of the sword.</b> Lest you say: one who died his own death defiles — the verse says so — but a slain by the sword does not defile, therefore it writes יטמא, 'shall defile,' seven days." "<b>Or a human bone [or a grave].</b> This verse proves that we do not require the entire corpse for defilement by contact." "<b>Or a human bone or a grave.</b> The verse was stringent regarding the impurity of a human being beyond all other impurities in the world, making it an 'father of fathers of impurity' (the highest grade), so that people would not linger among their dead out of fondness for them and grieve excessively; or so that they would not inquire of the dead and of ov mediums; and also for the sake of human dignity the verse was stringent about their impurity, so that they would not make waterskins and mats from their skin, and utensils from their bones to use — for this is a disgrace to human beings. And thus our Rabbis of blessed memory said: Why is human skin impure? So that they would not make the skin of their father and mother into mats. And why are human bones impure? So that a person would not make the bones of his father and mother into spoons. According to their fondness is their impurity."], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:18:44.665454674Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}